
All Cisco experiments (Expt 11, 12, 13, and 14) are run inside Cisco Packet
Tracer (a free network simulator used in college labs).

Here is the complete, beginner-friendly guide explaining where to run them, how
to set them up, and step-by-step instructions for each experiment.

Part 1: Where and How to Run

1.  Software to use: Cisco Packet Tracer
      - If you are in your college lab, it is already installed on your PC
        (search for "Packet Tracer" in the start menu/app drawer).
      - If on your own laptop, download it for free from Cisco Skills For All /
        NetAcad.
2.  How to access a router's console (CLI):
      - Whenever you place a router on the workspace, click on the router icon.
      - In the popup window that appears, click the CLI tab at the top.
      - Press Enter.
      - If it asks: Continue with configuration dialog? [yes/no]: \rightarrow
        type no and press Enter.
      - You will see the prompt: Router> (This is where you type commands).

Part 2: Step-by-Step for Experiment 11 (Familiarizing Commands)

You only need one router for this experiment.

Step 1: Place a Router

1.  Look at the bottom-left corner of Packet Tracer.
2.  Click on the round Routers icon.
3.  Select Router-PT (or 2811) and drag it onto the white workspace.

Step 2: Open the CLI and Run the Commands

Click the router \rightarrow click the CLI tab \rightarrow press Enter. Now type
these commands line-by-line:

Router> enable

(a) Prompt changes to Router#. You are now in Privileged Mode.

Router# configure terminal

(b) Prompt changes to Router(config)#. You are now in Global Configuration Mode.

Router(config)# exit
Router# show version

(c) Displays router hardware, IOS version, and memory. Press Spacebar until it
finishes.

Router# show ip protocols

(d) Shows if any routing protocol is active (currently none).

Router# show ip route

(e) Shows the current routing table.

Router# copy running-config startup-config

(f) Press Enter to confirm. This saves your changes.

Router# show history

(g) Shows your previously executed commands.

Router# show clock

(h) Displays the system time and date.

Router# show hosts

(i) Shows the host table / DNS cache.

Router# show ip interface brief

(j) Shows a clean summary of all interfaces and their IP status.

Router# show controllers serial 0/0

(k) Shows whether the port is configured as DCE or DTE.

Router# configure terminal
Router(config)# interface FastEthernet0/0
Router(config-if)# ip address 172.16.10.1 255.255.255.0
Router(config-if)# no shutdown
Router(config-if)# exit

Router(config)# interface Serial0/0
Router(config-if)# ip address 172.16.20.1 255.255.255.0
Router(config-if)# clock rate 64000
Router(config-if)# no shutdown
Router(config-if)# end

(l) Configures the Ethernet and Serial interfaces and brings them UP.

Part 3: Building the Topology for Experiments 12, 13, and 14

All three experiments (Static, RIPv2, OSPF) share the exact same physical
network shown in Figure 1:

[2621A] ---- (172.16.10.0/24) ---- [2501A] ~~~~ (172.16.20.0/24) ~~~~ [2501B] ~~~~ (172.16.40.0/24) ~~~~ [2501C]
                                                                          |                              |
                                                                    172.16.30.0/24                 172.16.50.0/24

(Note: ---- is Ethernet, ~~~~ is Serial)

Step 1: Place 4 Routers

  - In the bottom-left panel, select Router-PT (using Router-PT is best because
    it comes with FastEthernet and Serial ports built-in).
  - Drag 4 routers into a horizontal line:
      - Name Router 0: 2621A
      - Name Router 1: 2501A
      - Name Router 2: 2501B
      - Name Router 3: 2501C

Step 2: Connect the Cables

At the bottom-left, click the Lightning Bolt (Connections) icon:

1.  Between 2621A and 2501A:
      - Pick the Copper Cross-Over cable (dotted black line).
      - Click 2621A \rightarrow select FastEthernet0/0.
      - Click 2501A \rightarrow select FastEthernet0/0 (representing E0).
2.  Between 2501A and 2501B:
      - Pick the Serial DCE cable (red cable with a little clock icon).
      - Click 2501A \rightarrow select Serial2/0 (representing S0).
      - Click 2501B \rightarrow select Serial2/0 (representing S0).
3.  Between 2501B and 2501C:
      - Pick the Serial DCE cable again.
      - Click 2501B \rightarrow select Serial3/0 (representing S1).
      - Click 2501C \rightarrow select Serial2/0 (representing S0).
4.  For the LANs on 2501B (172.16.30.0) and 2501C (172.16.50.0):
      - In Packet Tracer, you can either configure a Loopback interface or
        configure their FastEthernet0/0 ports.

Step 3: Base IP Configuration on All 4 Routers

Open the CLI of each router and paste the following commands to assign the
addresses from the table:

On Router 2621A:

enable
configure terminal
hostname 2621A
interface FastEthernet0/0
 ip address 172.16.10.1 255.255.255.0
 no shutdown
exit

On Router 2501A:

enable
configure terminal
hostname 2501A
interface FastEthernet0/0
 ip address 172.16.10.2 255.255.255.0
 no shutdown
exit
interface Serial2/0
 ip address 172.16.20.1 255.255.255.0
 clock rate 64000
 no shutdown
exit

On Router 2501B:

enable
configure terminal
hostname 2501B
interface FastEthernet0/0
 ip address 172.16.30.1 255.255.255.0
 no shutdown
exit
interface Serial2/0
 ip address 172.16.20.2 255.255.255.0
 no shutdown
exit
interface Serial3/0
 ip address 172.16.40.1 255.255.255.0
 clock rate 64000
 no shutdown
exit

On Router 2501C:

enable
configure terminal
hostname 2501C
interface Serial2/0
 ip address 172.16.40.2 255.255.255.0
 no shutdown
exit
interface FastEthernet0/0
 ip address 172.16.50.1 255.255.255.0
 no shutdown
exit

(All link lights will now turn green).

Part 4: How to do Experiment 12 (Static Routing)

In static routing, each router must be told how to reach networks it is not
directly connected to: ip route <target-network> <subnet-mask> <next-hop-ip>

1. Enter these commands on each router:

  - On 2621A:

enable
configure terminal
ip route 172.16.20.0 255.255.255.0 172.16.10.2
ip route 172.16.30.0 255.255.255.0 172.16.10.2
ip route 172.16.40.0 255.255.255.0 172.16.10.2
ip route 172.16.50.0 255.255.255.0 172.16.10.2
end

  - On 2501A:

enable
configure terminal
ip route 172.16.30.0 255.255.255.0 172.16.20.2
ip route 172.16.40.0 255.255.255.0 172.16.20.2
ip route 172.16.50.0 255.255.255.0 172.16.20.2
end

  - On 2501B:

enable
configure terminal
ip route 172.16.10.0 255.255.255.0 172.16.20.1
ip route 172.16.50.0 255.255.255.0 172.16.40.2
end

  - On 2501C:

enable
configure terminal
ip route 172.16.10.0 255.255.255.0 172.16.40.1
ip route 172.16.20.0 255.255.255.0 172.16.40.1
ip route 172.16.30.0 255.255.255.0 172.16.40.1
end

2. Verify:

  - On 2621A, type: show ip route (routes marked with S will appear).
  - Type: ping 172.16.50.1
  - You should see: Success rate is 100 percent (5/5), !!!!!

Part 5: How to do Experiment 13 (RIPv2 Routing)

Tip: If continuing from Experiment 12, restart the routers or start a new file
with the Base IPs from Part 3 so old static routes don't interfere.

Open CLI on each router and run:

  - On 2621A:

enable
configure terminal
router rip
 version 2
 no auto-summary
 network 172.16.0.0
end

  - On 2501A:

enable
configure terminal
router rip
 version 2
 no auto-summary
 network 172.16.0.0
end

  - On 2501B:

enable
configure terminal
router rip
 version 2
 no auto-summary
 network 172.16.0.0
end

  - On 2501C:

enable
configure terminal
router rip
 version 2
 no auto-summary
 network 172.16.0.0
end

Verify:

1.  Click the Fast Forward Time button (bottom bar, looks like >>) 3–4 times to
    allow RIP updates to propagate.
2.  On 2621A, run: show ip route (routes marked with R will appear).
3.  Run: ping 172.16.50.1 (should reply !!!!!).

Part 6: How to do Experiment 14 (OSPF Routing)

Tip: Clear RIP routes (no router rip) or use a clean topology with Base IPs.

Run OSPF on all directly connected subnets in Area 0:

  - On 2621A:

enable
configure terminal
router ospf 1
 network 172.16.10.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
end

  - On 2501A:

enable
configure terminal
router ospf 1
 network 172.16.10.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
 network 172.16.20.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
end

  - On 2501B:

enable
configure terminal
router ospf 1
 network 172.16.20.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
 network 172.16.30.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
 network 172.16.40.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
end

  - On 2501C:

enable
configure terminal
router ospf 1
 network 172.16.40.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
 network 172.16.50.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
end

Verify:

1.  Wait a few seconds for neighbor adjacencies to form (messages like
    OSPF-5-ADJCHG: Process 1, Nbr ... to FULL will appear).
2.  On 2621A, run: show ip route (routes marked with O will appear).
3.  Run: ping 172.16.50.1 (should reply with !!!!!).
